# Mainstream browse

Mainstream browse organises a topic hierarchy into parent and child pages. [Official publishing guidance](https://design-guide.publishing.service.gov.uk/frontend-templates/mainstream-browse/).

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation; they do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## When to use it

Use the principle for a large household help collection or documentation catalogue. Start with subjects people recognise, such as lighting, heating or maintaining the system. Keep broad subjects distinct from detailed task links. A small household may only need a single flat list.

## Structure and behaviour

Provide a short introduction and plainly labelled topic links. Use a clear second level only where it reduces scanning effort. Alphabetical order can help a known-item list; task priority can help a curated list. Explain the chosen ordering and keep it stable.

## Home Assistant boundary

Do not import the government taxonomy or analytics-derived popularity lists. The theme does not change native HA areas, floors or labels. Navigation must use configured routes and avoid suggesting that an entity has only one possible organisational relationship.

